The {{nihongo|'''Chūō Line'''|中央線|Chūō-sen}} is a subway line in [[Okami|Okami (大紙)]], [[Okaiken]], operated by [[Okami Metro]]. True to its name (''Chūō'' means "Central"), it serves as the central east-west axis of the city's transport grid, running directly through the heart of the government and commercial districts. On system maps, the line is color-coded forest green ({{Okami Metro color|C}}), and its stations use the prefix "C".

The Chūō Line is strategically critical as the primary rail access to the Okami Bay Area, connecting the mainland to the artificial islands of Yumeshima (site of the upcoming [[Okami World Expo 2030]]) and Cosmosquare. To the east, it connects seamlessly with the [[Okaden]] (Okazaki Electric Railway), providing through-service to the [[Okazaki Prefecture]] suburbs.



==History==
Construction of the Chūō Line began in the mid-1960s to support the city's eastward expansion and the development of the bay area. The initial section between [[Bentencho-Central Station|Bentencho-Central]] and [[Tanimachi-Yonchome Station|Tanimachi-Yonchome]] opened on December 15, 1967.

The line was progressively extended west toward the port and east toward the mountains throughout the 1970s and 80s. The eastern terminus at [[Nagata-Okazaki Station|Nagata-Okazaki]] opened in 1985, commencing immediate through-service with the [[Okaden]] railway.

In 2024, the western extension from [[Cosmosquare Station|Cosmosquare]] to [[Yumeshima Station|Yumeshima]] was inaugurated, marking the completion of the "Expo Access Project." This extension features a unique undersea tunnel section and serves as the gateway to the Integrated Resort (IR) and Expo site.

